nce (company disclosures, n2):**\n  - Backlog was C$5.1B at H1 2026, about 1.2 times the guidance.\n  - Orders grew 11% year on year, and cancellations were under 1%.\n  - Management says the backlog covers the guidance. That claim is unverified.\n- **The weak Q2 2026 is mostly a one-off.** Revenue fell to C$890M because a fire stopped the Halbrook plant for nine weeks. Halbrook builds all of Voltaris's large power transformers. It restarted in August at full capacity, and delayed orders should shift into late 2026 and 2027.\n- **Against (industry data, n3):**\n  - Industry shipments fell 4% in Q2 2026 after growing earlier. n3 reads this as softness continuing into H2 2026.\n  - The trailing four quarters sum to about C$3.73B. Normal quarters of C$960–980M annualise to about C$3.85–3.9B. Reaching C$4.2B needs roughly 8–13% growth, against a shrinking industry.\n- **The industry decline may be less informative than n3 thought.** Voltaris's own Q2 dip is explained by the fire, and its orders are growing. That points to share gains or strength in its utility and data-centre segments.\n- **What could change this:**\n  - The CEA maker table was unavailable, so I couldn't check Voltaris's market share.\n  - Backlog may not convert to revenue within 2027.\n  - Halbrook remains a single point of failure.\n  - Q3 2026 revenue is the best near-term check. A figure of about C$1.0B or more would support guidance, and one near C$900M would not.\n\nDetail is in n2.r1 and n3.r1.","ttft_ms":1798,"type":"result","duration_ms":68094,"uuid":"64540b07-6a45-4021-8302-fd84d16fdc8f","ttft_stream_ms":1136,"time_to_request_ms":100,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":6,"system_prompt":4,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":2,"input_attachments":22,"input_hooks":12,"conversation_append":6,"transcript_persist":4,"tool_pool_refresh":1,"init_message":10,"init_frame_send":4,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":5,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":2,"request_announcements":6,"query_